# How to Use This Book

## What this book is for

Every CSS Gender Studies answer is judged partly on **evidence**: the right date, the right law with its section, the right figure with its source and year, the right thinker with the right book. This book collects all of that evidence from the seven topic Knowledge Bases into one volume, so that you can check a fact in seconds while revising — and so that the facts you quote are **consistent** across every answer you write.

::: table | The parts of this book.
@widths 3.6,10.4
Part | What it gives you
One — Master Timeline | About 95 landmark events from 1792 to 2026, in eight eras, each tagged with its topic
Two — Topic by Topic | Each topic's own timeline and tables: figures, theories, comparisons and quotations
Three — Numbers Card | Every statistic worth quoting, with its source and year; volatile figures marked ★
Four — Index of Laws | The Constitution's articles on women; every Pakistani law on women with key sections; international instruments
Five — Index of Thinkers | About 115 thinkers, their key works and the one idea to remember
Six — Conferences, Reports and Surveys | The UN conferences, global reports and Pakistani surveys behind the data
Seven — Cases and Case Studies | Court judgements, incidents and the three syllabus case studies
Eight — Pakistani Firsts | Women "firsts" in politics, the state and public life — MCQ favourites
:::

## How facts are marked

1. **Source and year, always.** A figure without a source and a year loses marks. Write "female labour-force participation was **24.4%** (Labour Force Survey 2024–25)", not "about a quarter".
2. **★ means volatile.** Figures marked ★ in the Numbers Card change every year (rankings, survey results, case counts). They were checked in **October 2026**. If a newer report appears before your exam, use it — and say which year it describes.
3. **"As stated in the paper."** Some past MCQs quote figures from the paper itself that differ from current data (for example, female literacy "48%" in 2019, or reserved seats "17%" in 2016). In an MCQ, choose the figure the stem attributes; in a descriptive answer, use the latest verified figure.
4. **Dates of laws.** A Pakistani law is dated by the year it was passed (the Punjab Act of ==2016== was passed on 24 February and published on 29 February). Where an ordinance was later made an Act, both years are given (*Qisas* and *Diyat*: Ordinance ==1990==, Act ==1997==).
5. **Ranges, not false precision.** Where sources disagree (for example, the share of intersex births), quote the range and the sources.

::: eye | How to use this book in the last month
Use **Part Three (Numbers Card)** and **Part Four (Laws)** daily: cover the right-hand column and test yourself. Before writing any practice answer, list three facts from this book you will use — a date, a law and a figure with its source. In the exam hall, one precise fact per heading is worth more than a paragraph of general argument.
:::
